---
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import * as ex from '../data/examples';
import { coreFile, coreTree, specs, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Examples"
  description="Valid and invalid RoboLang task files, goals, branching plans, symbolic simulations, goal delegation and the Core 0.1 reference simulation, each shown with the real output of the robolang command."
>
  <PageHead slot="head" index="04" label="Examples" title="Examples">
    Task files and what the tools say about them. Every output on this page was produced by the real <code>robolang</code> command, distribution {versions.distribution}.
  </PageHead>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      These examples show how tasks are written, checked and simulated. Simulation is symbolic and
      nothing here runs on a robot. A task that validates, or succeeds in simulation, is not thereby
      safe to run on a robot.
    </p>

    <h2 id="pick-and-place">Pick and place</h2>
    <p>
      This is <a href={coreFile('examples/pick_and_place.rob')}><code>examples/pick_and_place.rob</code></a>
      from the core repository, unchanged. It declares five typed parameters, one safety annotation,
      two preconditions and a five-step plan.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="rob" code={ex.pickAndPlaceSource} label="examples/pick_and_place.rob" />

    <h3>Validate</h3>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang validate examples/pick_and_place.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.pickAndPlaceValidate} label="Output (exit status 0)" />
    <p>
      The file is valid, with one warning. <code>W401</code> points at line 5, column 23: the
      <code>max_speed</code> limit has no unit. A unitless limit is deprecated in language {
        versions.language
      } and, as the message says, is never treated as an enforced limit. The example is kept as it is
      so that files written for the earlier version of RoboLang stay valid.
    </p>

    <h3>Inspect</h3>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang inspect examples/pick_and_place.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.pickAndPlaceInspect} label="Output" />

    <h3 id="json-ir">Inspect as JSON IR</h3>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang inspect examples/pick_and_place.rob --json" />
    <CodeBlock lang="json" code={ex.pickAndPlaceJson} label="Output (line breaks condensed for display)" />
    <p>
      The intermediate representation names its schema and version, so a consuming program can check
      what it has been given. Notice that the unitless limit appears with
      <code>"unit": null</code> and that the number is kept as the text <code>"0.5"</code>. The fields
      are defined in the <a href={specs.irSchema}>IR schema specification</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="simulate">Simulate</h2>
    <p>
      The same file, run against the world file
      <a href={coreFile('tests/fixtures/simulation/worlds/base.json')}><code>base.json</code></a>
      from the core repository. It binds <code>r</code> to a ready robot <code>arm1</code> and
      declares the region <code>cell</code> clear.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang simulate examples/pick_and_place.rob --world tests/fixtures/simulation/worlds/base.json"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.pickAndPlaceSimulate} label="Standard error (exit status 0)" />
    <CodeBlock
      lang="json"
      code={ex.pickAndPlaceSimulateRecord}
      label="Standard output: selected fields of the run record, line breaks condensed"
    />
    <p>
      The record shows each executor state in order. The unitless speed limit is reported as
      <code>unit_unspecified_not_enforced</code>: checked, recorded and never enforced. The full
      record also lists every step and the final world; its fields are defined in the
      <a href={specs.simulation}>simulation specification</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="fail-closed">A run that fails closed</h3>
    <p>
      With <a href={coreFile('tests/fixtures/simulation/worlds/not_ready.json')}><code>not_ready.json</code></a>,
      the robot is not ready. The first precondition is false, so the run stops before any step.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang simulate examples/pick_and_place.rob --world tests/fixtures/simulation/worlds/not_ready.json"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.pickAndPlaceNotReady} label="Standard error (exit status 4)" />
    <CodeBlock
      lang="json"
      code={ex.pickAndPlaceNotReadyRecord}
      label="Standard output: selected fields of the run record, line breaks condensed"
    />

    <h2 id="inspect-part">Inspection with a wait</h2>
    <p>
      This example was written for this page. It starts with the optional version marker, gives the
      speed limit a unit, and uses the remaining statement and condition forms: <code>inspect</code>,
      both kinds of <code>wait</code>, <code>object_exists</code> and <code>sensor_ok</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="rob" code={ex.inspectPartSource} label="inspect_part.rob" />
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang validate inspect_part.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.inspectPartValidate} label="Output (exit status 0)" />
    <p>With a unit on the limit there is no warning.</p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang inspect inspect_part.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.inspectPartInspect} label="Output" />
    <p>
      The tool states that the limit is "declared only; not enforced by this tool". That is the
      accurate reading of every <code>@safety</code> annotation today.
    </p>

    <h2 id="combined">Combined conditions (language 1.2)</h2>
    <p>
      This is <a href={coreFile('tests/fixtures/v1.2/simulation/sources/pre_or.rob')}><code>pre_or.rob</code></a>
      from the core repository's tests, unchanged. The marker <code>robolang 1.2;</code> makes it a 1.2
      file, and its precondition holds if either region is clear.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="rob" code={ex.preOrSource} label="tests/fixtures/v1.2/simulation/sources/pre_or.rob" />
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang validate tests/fixtures/v1.2/simulation/sources/pre_or.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.preOrValidate} label="Output (exit status 0)" />
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang inspect tests/fixtures/v1.2/simulation/sources/pre_or.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.preOrInspect} label="Output (exit status 0)" />
    <p>
      <code>inspect</code> shows a combined condition in source form. A group inside another
      operator is shown in parentheses, because <code>and</code> and <code>or</code> are never mixed
      without them.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang inspect tests/fixtures/v1.2/simulation/sources/pre_or.rob --json" />
    <CodeBlock lang="json" code={ex.preOrJson} label="Output (line breaks condensed for display)" />
    <p>
      A 1.2 source produces IR schema 1.1, where a combined condition is a node with a
      <code>kind</code> of <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> or <code>not</code>. The fields are
      defined in the <a href={specs.irSchema11}>IR schema 1.1 specification</a>.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang simulate tests/fixtures/v1.2/simulation/sources/pre_or.rob --world tests/fixtures/v1.2/simulation/worlds/base.json"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.preOrSimulate} label="Standard error (exit status 0)" />
    <CodeBlock
      lang="json"
      code={ex.preOrSimulateRecord}
      label="Standard output: selected fields of the run record, line breaks condensed"
    />

    <h2 id="goals">Goals decide success (language 1.3)</h2>
    <p>
      This is <a href={coreFile('examples/handover.rob')}><code>examples/handover.rob</code></a> from
      the core repository, unchanged. The <code>goal</code> block says what must be true when the task
      is done; the plan is only the way there.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="rob" code={ex.handoverSource} label="examples/handover.rob" />
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang validate examples/handover.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.handoverValidate} label="Output (exit status 0)" />
    <p>
      Its parameters are bound in
      <a href={coreFile('examples/world.json')}><code>examples/world.json</code></a>, where the
      handoff zone becomes clear after 5 seconds.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang simulate examples/handover.rob --world examples/world.json" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.handoverSimulate} label="Standard error (exit status 0)" />
    <CodeBlock
      lang="json"
      code={ex.handoverSimulateRecord}
      label="Standard output: selected fields of the run record"
    />
    <p>
      Every 1.3 run record ends with <code>core_outcome</code>: the same verdict as a Core
      {versions.core} outcome, citing signed observations of the final world as its evidence.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="json"
      code={ex.handoverCoreOutcome}
      label="Standard output: the outcome in core_outcome, evidence list omitted"
    />

    <h3 id="goal-false">Every step succeeds, the goal does not</h3>
    <p>
      This example was written for this page. The goal asks for the box at <code>dst</code> and the
      robot back at <code>src</code>, but the plan never returns.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="rob" code={ex.goalMissSource} label="deliver_and_return.rob" />
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang simulate deliver_and_return.rob --world examples/world.json --bind r=arm1 box=box1 src=shelf dst=table"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.goalMissSimulate} label="Standard error (exit status 4)" />
    <CodeBlock
      lang="json"
      code={ex.goalMissRecord}
      label="Standard output: selected fields of the run record, each step cut down to its index and status"
    />
    <p>
      All four steps succeeded, yet the run is <code>FAILED</code> with <code>S301</code>: a goal
      condition is false in the final world. An unknown goal condition fails the run too, with
      <code>S302</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="branching">Branching (language 1.4)</h2>
    <p>
      This is <a href={coreFile('tests/fixtures/v1.4/valid/branch_route.rob')}><code
        >branch_route.rob</code
      ></a> from the core repository's tests, unchanged. Each branch carries its own goal, the
      task goal applies to every path, and the last branch reports and stops with <code>fail</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="rob" code={ex.routeSource} label="tests/fixtures/v1.4/valid/branch_route.rob" />
    <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="robolang inspect tests/fixtures/v1.4/valid/branch_route.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.routeInspect} label="Output (exit status 0)" />
    <p>
      Run against <code>examples/world.json</code> with the region bound on the command line. With
      <code>cell</code>, which is clear, the first branch runs:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang simulate tests/fixtures/v1.4/valid/branch_route.rob --world examples/world.json --bind r=arm1 box=box1 src=shelf a=table b=handoff cell=cell"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.routeClearSimulate} label="Standard error (exit status 0)" />
    <CodeBlock
      lang="json"
      code={ex.routeClearRecord}
      label="Standard output: selected fields of the run record"
    />
    <p>
      With <code>handoff_zone</code>, which is not clear at the start, the run takes the
      <code>else if</code> branch and checks that branch's goal instead:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang simulate tests/fixtures/v1.4/valid/branch_route.rob --world examples/world.json --bind r=arm1 box=box1 src=shelf a=table b=handoff cell=handoff_zone"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.routeBlockedSimulate} label="Standard error (exit status 0)" />
    <CodeBlock
      lang="json"
      code={ex.routeBlockedRecord}
      label="Standard output: selected fields of the run record"
    />
    <p>
      The record lists every decision with the condition's result. A condition that is
      <code>unknown</code> fails the run with <code>S261</code>, and <code>fail</code> ends it with
      <code>S262</code>. The rules are in the <a href={specs.language14}>language 1.4
      specification</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="delegate">Delegating a goal (experimental)</h2>
    <p>
      <code>robolang delegate</code> sends the goal of <code>handover.rob</code>, not its plan, to the
      arm as a signed Core {versions.core} <code>propose_goal</code>. The arm looks in its own plan
      library, <a href={coreTree('examples/plans')}><code>examples/plans</code></a>, finds
      <code>relocate_box</code> with the same goal, runs it and reports an outcome. The planner
      accepts <code>succeeded</code> only after re-checking the signed evidence itself.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang delegate examples/handover.rob --world examples/world.json --from rl:agent:planner-1 --to rl:robot:arm1 --plans examples/plans"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.handoverDelegate} label="Standard error (exit status 0)" />
    <CodeBlock
      lang="json"
      code={ex.handoverDelegateRecord}
      label="Standard output: selected fields of the delegate record, each message cut down to its time, type and sender"
    />
    <p>
      A plan that needs a second robot brings it in as a peer, with a commitment, a lease and a
      custody transfer. This is simulation only: one process and simulation-only signatures. The
      rules are in the <a href={specs.delegate}>delegate specification</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="arch02">Reference simulation (experimental)</h2>
    <p>
      <code>robolang arch02</code> runs one case of the RFC-001 reference scenario with
      Core {versions.core} messages between an AI planner stub, a fixed arm and a mobile robot, in one
      process. The scenario and cases are in
      <a href={coreFile('tests/fixtures/arch02/README.md')}><code>tests/fixtures/arch02</code></a>.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang arch02 --scenario tests/fixtures/arch02/scenario.json --cases tests/fixtures/arch02/cases.json --case happy_path"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.arch02HappyPath} label="Standard error (exit status 0)" />
    <p>
      In the happy path the planner records the goal as succeeded, which Core {versions.core} allows
      only with a verification that cites observations. Case
      <code>v06_partition_before_release</code> cuts the link between the two robots during the
      custody handover:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="bash"
      code="robolang arch02 --scenario tests/fixtures/arch02/scenario.json --cases tests/fixtures/arch02/cases.json --case v06_partition_before_release"
    />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.arch02Partition} label="Standard error (exit status 0)" />
    <p>
      The planner records <code>unknown</code>, not success: custody of the container could not be
      confirmed. The full run record of each case, printed to standard output, lists every message
      and event. The rules are in the <a href={specs.referenceSimulation}>reference simulation
      specification</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="invalid">Invalid files</h2>
    <p>
      When a file is not valid, <code>validate</code> prints a diagnostic with a position and a code
      and returns exit status 1.
    </p>

    <h3>An undeclared name</h3>
    <p>The plan moves to <code>bench</code>, which is not a parameter of the task.</p>
    <CodeBlock lang="rob" code={ex.undeclaredSource} label="undeclared.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.undeclaredValidate} label="Output (exit status 1)" />

    <h3>A missing semicolon</h3>
    <CodeBlock lang="rob" code={ex.missingSemicolonSource} label="missing_semicolon.rob" />
    <CodeBlock code={ex.missingSemicolonValidate} label="Output (exit status 1)" />

    <p>
      The core repository has a larger set of valid and invalid files used by its own tests, in
      <a href={coreFile('tests/fixtures/README.md')}><code>tests/fixtures</code></a>. The meaning of
      each code is listed in the <a href={specs.language}>language specification</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
